[Music]  Americans spend an average of $1,100 per  person per year on prescription drugs  that's more than anyone else in the  world it's true Americans take a lot of  pills but the real difference for the  u.s. is the price of those drugs which  many warn are rapidly becoming  unaffordable President Donald Trump has  promised to bring drug prices down by  forcing drug makers to bid for the  government's business Pharma has a lot  of lobbies a lot of lobbyists and a lot  of power and there's very little bidding  on drugs here's the situation the  pharmaceutical industry has come under  fire for what critics have labeled price  gouging everybody is doing it it in  capitalism you try to get the highest  price for that you can't for a product  Martin shkreli it became the poster  child of greed when his company raised  the price of an old anti-parasitic drug  from 13 dollars to 750 dollars a pill  and in 2016  Milan pharmaceuticals CEO Heather brash  was grilled by Congress when her company  raised the price of an EpiPen more than  twofold since 2012 so how do drug prices  work in the US versus the rest of the  world in Europe governments negotiate  directly with drug makers to limit what  their state-funded health systems pay in  the u.s. drug company is more or less  set whatever price the market will bear  most of the costs are paid for either by  Medicare the government program for the  elderly or by private insurance while  Medicare legally can't negotiate drug  prices private insurers can private  payers typically rely on third party  pharmacy benefit managers or PPM's  like Express Scripts and CVS Caremark to  negotiate discounts PBMs often make  exclusive and secretive deals with drug  makers which can result in an insurance  company covering some drugs but not  others in the end Americans wind up  paying about 17 percent of drug costs  out of pocket so here's the argument  pharmaceutical companies say that high  profits are necessary to fund research  and development and that regulating drug  prices might slow medical advances  advocates of regulation say lower prices  wouldn't stop innovation and point out  that some drug companies spend more on  marketing than they do on R&amp;D

